        "tipo": "gramatica",
        "min": 14,
        "titulo": "Haben or sein? Step by step",
        "explicacao": "STEP 1: most verbs form the Perfekt with «haben». STEP 2: three groups use «SEIN» instead: (1) verbs of MOVEMENT from one place to another: gehen, fahren, kommen, fliegen, laufen, reisen, ankommen; (2) verbs of a CHANGE OF STATE: aufstehen, einschlafen, aufwachen, werden; (3) the verbs «sein» and «bleiben» themselves. STEP 3: rule of thumb: if you can add \"from A to B\" or say \"something changed\", use «sein»; otherwise, «haben». STEP 4: the auxiliary «sein» is in the present tense: ich bin, du bist, er ist, wir sind, ihr seid, sie sind. STEP 5: the verb bracket stays the same: «Ich bin nach Berlin gefahren.» (I drove to Berlin.) «Er ist um sieben Uhr aufgestanden.» (He got up at seven.) «Wir sind zu Hause geblieben.» (We stayed home.)",
        "comparacao": "English uses \"have\" for everything: \"I have gone\", \"she has stayed\". Older English still had the German split — \"He is come\", \"Christ is risen\", \"The leaves are fallen\" — and German keeps it: «haben» for most verbs, «sein» for going, coming and changing. So «Ich bin gegangen» is literally \"I am gone\".",
